00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:14.110
OK, so first topic today
is Kraft's inequality.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:15.910
What is Kraft's inequality?

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:18.400
So last time, we saw
that a prefix code

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:22.000
is equivalent to having
your codewords leaves

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.040
on a binary tree, right?

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:25.510
So when we say
prefix code, we mean

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:27.640
no code word is a
prefix of another leaves

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:28.690
of a binary tree.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:33.280
These are very English
representations of the thing,

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:36.220
not as mathematical as
some of you might like.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:37.960
Today, we will see
a characterization

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.020
of prefix codes, which
is very mathematical.

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:44.620
You can say a prefix code
is equivalent to satisfying

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:46.570
a certain inequality,
which we will just

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:48.850
see in the next slide.

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.760
And this will be very useful
for the rest of the lecture

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.490
and beyond.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:57.540
So this is the inequality.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.280
So the first part
is the forward part,

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:00.450
and then we have a converse.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:05.370
So given a prefix code with
certain codeword lengths--

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:08.910
oh, by the way, these will
be posted on the website.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:12.360
So, yeah, if you
want to take notes,

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:14.790
but if you don't,
then that's fine.

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:18.480
OK, so for any prefix
code in the world, that

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.370
has certain codeword lengths l1
to lk, the sum of this quantity

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:27.210
summation I equal to 1
to k, 2 to the minus li

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:30.840
is less than or equal to 1.

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:31.340
Right.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:33.480
And we'll see this come
up again and again.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.900
So for now, OK,
this is something.

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:38.180
And maybe the converse part
might interest some people

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:43.160
more, which is that given
any set of integers l1 to lk,

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:47.690
satisfying this inequality,
there exists a prefix code

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:50.000
with lengths l1 to lk, right?

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:51.710
So basically,
having a prefix code

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:53.660
is equivalent to satisfying
Kraft's inequality

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:58.580
on the lengths, so it's an
if and only if situation.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:01.990
Any questions before I move on?

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:03.865
We'll see some
examples in a second.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:07.340
OK.

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:09.620
And don't worry if it
looks very abstract.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.270
Why are we doing this?

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.370
As we move on, I think by
the end of this lecture,

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:17.200
you will have a very strong
intuition about some of this.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.060
Letter ink ran out here.

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:20.360
OK.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:21.890
Proof of the converse part.

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:25.940
So converse part says that if
you have a sequence of lengths

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:30.550
satisfying this inequality, then
you can create a prefix code.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:31.750
This I won't cover too much.

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:34.210
Basically, it's the same
construction as Shannon codes.

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:35.500
So you sort the lens.

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:38.590
You pick lens-- you pick
a codeword according

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:44.420
to the prefix condition, and
you get a code at the end.

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:44.920
Right.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:49.300
So let me not do an example.

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.370
I think it's the exact same
construction as the Shannon

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:52.870
code's.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.780
Yeah, feel free to revisit.

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.150
OK.

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:03.390
OK, the forward part, I will
do a proof sketch, which should

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:04.560
give you enough intuition.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.320
And maybe it will help
you understand the proof

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.720
from last lecture
better as well.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:11.920
So what do we need to prove?

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.200
We want to prove that
if you have a prefix

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:21.050
code with these lengths,
with lengths l1 to lk,

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:24.680
then this inequality
holds this one.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:28.070
Summation li to k, 2 to
the minus li, less than

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:28.730
or equal to 1.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:30.730
So this is what you need
to prove for any prefix

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:31.950
code in the world.

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:34.130
So how will we prove it?

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:37.860
We define something called lmax.

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.350
lmax is the maximum--

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:48.310
lmax is the maximum length,
maximum codeword length,

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:49.600
the longest codeword.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:51.910
OK.

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:54.160
So this time, I came
prepared with a diagram.

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:54.700
So, OK.

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:56.260
So let's see what we can.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:59.440
So I've drawn here the actual
code, all the code words

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:00.980
on a tree.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:04.160
And I've shown the depths
of the different leaves.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:07.490
So l1, l2, l3 are
at the same depth.

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:10.520
L4 is this the deepest
node in the tree,

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:15.260
which is same as lmax, because
that's the deepest one, right?

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:19.340
Now this is something, I
think, we saw last lecture,

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:26.330
that 2 power lmax minus li
is the number of descendants

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:31.180
of the ith codeword
at depth lmax, right?

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:32.320
So we saw this last time.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:33.528
I won't dwell on it too much.

00:15:33.528 --> 00:15:36.250
Basically, the idea is if you
have a binary tree of depth n,

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:39.830
then the number of leaves
is 2 to the n, right?

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:42.590
So if you are at
depth li already,

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:46.430
and then you need to go to lmax,
so the binary tree starting

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:50.420
at li up to lmax has number
of leaves 2 to the power,

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:52.760
like the difference
in the heights.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:54.950
So let me illustrate it here.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:56.870
Like, if you see at
this part, right?

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:58.190
So this is l1.

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:00.050
This is the node l1.

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:04.490
And this is the subtree
that you can create,

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:06.830
which is the
children of this guy.

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:10.160
And this has number
of nodes, which

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.980
is 2 to the lmax minus
l number of leaves,

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:17.430
and so on for this guy, so
on for this guy, and so on.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:29.390
OK, the other property we will
use is that if you have node i

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:33.082
and node j, their
descendants cannot overlap.

00:16:33.082 --> 00:16:34.040
Why can't they overlap?

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:36.335
Because prefix-free
property, right?

00:16:36.335 --> 00:16:38.210
So you can see it, and
sometimes, it's easier

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:39.170
to see in the picture, right?

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:40.820
Like, clearly, these
guys can't overlap

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:45.000
because they are just like
separate branches of the tree.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:48.120
So therefore, if you
add all of these up,

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.670
you add all of these guys,
who are at depth lmax.

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:57.580
Some will be lower than the
total size of the number

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:00.520
of nodes at depth lmax.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:03.763
So we're using a
very simple property,

00:17:03.763 --> 00:17:05.680
where if you have a bunch
of disjoint subsets,

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:08.770
and you add up their sizes, that
cannot be more than the total

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:11.589
size of the entire set, right?

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:14.560
So summation of this thing is
less than or equal l2 to lmax.

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:20.990
Yeah, let me give you a
minute to absorb this.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:23.540
And if you have any
questions, please ask.

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.270
Yes?

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:44.740
Are these the number of branches
before you get to a leaf?

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:45.240
Yeah.

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:45.740
Yeah.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:48.900
So it's-- yeah, it's the
depth, or it's the length

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:49.920
of the codeword.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:51.030
That's the way to think.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:52.300
Yeah.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:56.820
So l1 is 1, because
this is 1, right?

00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:02.220
l2, in this example, is 3
because there are three things.

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:07.575
Yeah.

00:18:07.575 --> 00:18:08.950
I think this is
the last time you

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:11.320
will see this tree-style proof.

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:13.930
So if you don't like
it, this is the end.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:22.170
OK, let me move on.

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:22.670
OK.

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:23.545
We'll go to the quiz.

00:18:23.545 --> 00:18:27.620
Before that, let me just say
that last lecture, if you

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:29.413
remember, we talked
about prefix codes,

00:18:29.413 --> 00:18:31.580
but we also talked about a
bigger category of codes,

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:33.530
called uniquely
decodable codes which

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:37.350
were basically lossless codes.

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:40.020
And here, as you see, we are
proving Kraft's inequality

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:41.310
for prefix codes.

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:43.920
In the homework, you will
prove Kraft's inequality

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:45.720
for uniquely
decodable codes, which

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:47.250
is just a more
powerful statement,

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:49.170
because prefix codes
are a subset of uniquely

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.600
decodable codes.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:53.610
And that's a very
different proof style,

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:56.460
so I hope you enjoy
that one as well.

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:01.450
OK, let's do the quiz questions.

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:04.500
And here, we will do some
examples of Kraft's inequality

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:05.000
as well.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.900
So this will help you get
familiar with the thing.

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:11.880
OK.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:14.610
I think, here, I will
ask for your help.

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:18.300
So here, the question was,
you were given a distribution

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:20.190
over A, B, C, D, E.

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:22.500
And you wanted to
build a Shannon code

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:23.880
for this distribution.

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:27.540
So you could just use that
Shannon code construction

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:28.530
algorithm.

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:31.010
So can somebody help me with--

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:33.840
I already computed the
lengths of the codewords

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:35.070
with this formula, right?

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:37.350
So now, you only need
to tell me the codewords

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:38.760
with these lengths.

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:40.530
Please, anybody?

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:41.030
A?

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:45.340
OK, someone says it's 00.

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:47.110
Yeah, so the
algorithm is you just

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:50.920
pick any available codeword
at that length, right?

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:54.880
And available means that
it shouldn't be a suffix

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:57.430
or prefix of anybody else.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:19:58.535
OK.

00:19:58.535 --> 00:19:59.035
B?

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:03.840
OK, here, 01 and 10.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:05.850
Let's do 01.

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:07.110
Keep it simple this time.

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:12.120
OK, C. OK, I see 1011.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:16.590
Again, take 10, so easier,
more lexicographic.

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:22.020
OK, D. OK, D, let's
do 110, right?

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:24.690
So we could-- for example,
we couldn't choose 100

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:26.730
because that would
be like C would be,

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:29.370
then it would violate
the prefix-free property.

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.360
But 110 is totally fine.

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:31.860
E?

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:39.790
Yeah, so you see, you could
have chosen 111 in a good code,

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:42.460
but a Shannon code must
have these lengths.

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:44.770
So it's like
suboptimal in that way.

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:47.770
So we have to choose a
codeword with length four.

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:52.870
So let's choose 1110, which
will satisfy all the prefix

00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:54.910
Properties

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:56.450
OK.

00:20:56.450 --> 00:21:00.140
And the expected length
that was question 2.

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:02.420
I hope this is very easy now.

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:03.680
So you just do the math.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:10.257
You get the 2.37.

00:21:10.257 --> 00:21:12.590
OK, this is something that
should have been on the quiz,

00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:14.840
but we couldn't add, because
I didn't finish on time.

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:20.260
We just saw that Kraft
inequality, 2 power minus li,

00:21:20.260 --> 00:21:22.010
the sum should be less
than or equal to 1.

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:27.240
So if we compute that
sum for these lengths,

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.890
you can do the math.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:31.500
You get 15/16, which
is less than 1.

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:32.400
So that's good.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:35.370
You are satisfying the
Kraft's inequality.

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:38.760
And there is a gap actually,
like it's not equal to 1 here.

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:43.770
And we'll come back to
that gap in a second.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:44.460
Right.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:45.590
I hope this is very clear.

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:47.340
So 2 power minus li,
you are summing over.

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:49.170
So 2 power minus 2
plus 2 power minus 2

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:51.240
plus two power minus
2 plus 2 power minus 3

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:52.560
plus 2 power minus 4.

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:54.150
That's the math here.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:57.930
And the total is 15/16,
which is less than 1.

00:21:57.930 --> 00:22:01.660
Any prefix code will always
satisfy the Kraft's inequality.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:02.160
OK.

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:09.700
Then we asked for a better code,
and I already made it at home.

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:14.020
So as some of you, I think,
suggested that the last length

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:15.190
need not be 4, right?

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:16.690
This is clearly suboptimal.

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:19.840
You could have
chosen length 3 here.

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:21.190
Is that clear to everyone?

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:23.260
That you could have chosen
length 3 here, right?

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:27.820
Because 110 is also not a
prefix or suffix of anything.

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:32.110
OK, now if you compute
the expected length,

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:35.830
you actually get 2.25, which
is strictly lower than the 2.37

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:38.020
we got for the
Shannon code, right?

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:40.660
So Shannon code is a good code,
but it's not the optimal code.

00:22:40.660 --> 00:22:42.035
In this case, you
can [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:42.035 --> 00:22:44.620
better than the Shannon code.

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:49.780
And in fact, if you now compute
the Kraft sum, that 1 over 2

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:54.390
to the l, and you sum it over,
you actually get 1, right?

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.970
So you will see,
in the next lecture

00:22:56.970 --> 00:23:02.020
that an optimal code
satisfies Kraft's inequality

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:03.340
with equality.

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:05.890
For an optimal code, the
summation of 2 power minus li

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:08.170
is actually equal to 1.

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:11.050
So actually, the gap
between 1 and your Kraft sum

00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:13.870
tells you how far you
are from the optimality.

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:19.230
OK, any questions on question 1?

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:20.910
I think most of
you got it, right?

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:24.460
From what I remember.

00:23:24.460 --> 00:23:26.150
OK, good.

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:27.890
Second was, I think, very easy.

00:23:27.890 --> 00:23:31.900
I hope let's quickly
solve it together.

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.910
We just asked, are
these codes prefix-free?

00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:35.770
Was 2.1 prefix-free?

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:42.820
2.1, some people
are saying no, and I

00:23:42.820 --> 00:23:49.810
think it is not right because,
see, D is a prefix of--

00:23:49.810 --> 00:23:54.550
D is a prefix of E,
so clearly no, OK?

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:56.080
I hope that's clear.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:24:02.180
OK, let's compute the
Kraft sum for this guy.

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:06.700
So it's like, you
have four terms, which

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:09.400
are like 1/2 square, because
you have four things which

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:10.510
have length 2.

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:16.190
And then you have one term,
which has length 3, right?

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:18.320
So this is just
2 power minus li.

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:20.630
I am summing over
all the codewords.

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:25.140
And if you do the math,
this will be more than--

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:27.000
it's like 1 plus 1/8.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:28.830
So it's greater than 1, right?

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:31.740
So we saw that any
prefix code has

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:33.035
to satisfy Kraft's inequality.

00:24:33.035 --> 00:24:35.160
So if your code doesn't
satisfy Kraft's inequality,

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:36.750
it cannot be a
prefix code, right?

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:40.292
So another way to check.

00:24:40.292 --> 00:24:42.250
OK, just by looking at
the lens, you don't even

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:43.150
need to see the codewords.

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:44.620
Just by looking at
the lens, you know

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:45.787
that it's not a prefix code.

00:24:45.787 --> 00:24:48.190
It cannot be a prefix code, OK?

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:48.910
Is that clear?

00:24:48.910 --> 00:24:50.770
That Kraft's inequality
basically gives you

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:54.430
a way to identify prefix codes.

00:24:54.430 --> 00:24:57.110
OK, 2.2.

00:24:57.110 --> 00:24:59.210
So you have these five.

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:00.575
Was this a prefix code?

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:05.720
OK, people are nodding.

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:06.220
Good.

00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:09.290
Yes, it was a very
easy to check again.

00:25:09.290 --> 00:25:13.450
And if you compute the
Kraft's sum, which I will--

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:17.370
I think it's 5/8, so this
is less than or equal to 1,

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:18.300
so good.

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:22.090
It satisfies the
Kraft's inequality.

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:23.260
How about 2.3?

00:25:23.260 --> 00:25:26.050
Is that a prefix code?

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:29.060
Yeah, people are saying no.

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:32.090
And they are correct, because
these two have the same,

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:33.830
so it's a very bad code.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.540
OK.

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:37.820
Now if you compute the
Kraft sum for this guy,

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:46.290
it's like 3 divided by 4 plus 2
divided by 8, which is like 1.

00:25:46.290 --> 00:25:47.910
So how is this possible, right?

00:25:47.910 --> 00:25:49.800
So this satisfies
Kraft's inequality.

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:53.970
The sum is less
than or equal to 1.

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:55.800
But it's not a prefix code.

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:58.560
Can you square that with
the results we just saw?

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:03.348
You could just
switch [INAUDIBLE]..

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:08.910
I think you are on
the right track.

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:11.010
Kraft's inequality,
what it says is

00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:14.490
that if your lengths satisfy
the Kraft's inequality,

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:16.285
then you can--

00:26:16.285 --> 00:26:17.910
yeah, then a prefix
code exists, right?

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:20.220
But your particular code
need not be a prefix code.

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:23.010
You could just make a code,
where everything is zero.

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:24.630
So clearly, it's
not a prefix code.

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:29.560
But you could make a code out
of just by slight modification

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:33.160
of this, just make 01, and
then it becomes a prefix code,

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:33.670
right?

00:26:33.670 --> 00:26:35.860
So if Kraft's
inequality is satisfied,

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:37.510
you will get a prefix code.

00:26:37.510 --> 00:26:40.580
But your particular code
may not be that prefix code.

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:45.215
It could not-- it might
just be not a prefix code.

00:26:45.215 --> 00:26:45.715
OK.

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:51.180
OK, good.

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:55.260
OK, let's move on
the exciting parts.

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:56.340
So what will we do?

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:58.830
We will talk about some
information theoretic

00:26:58.830 --> 00:26:59.395
quantities.

00:26:59.395 --> 00:27:01.020
If you do an information
theory course,

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:03.120
you will see much more on this.

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:08.310
We will just do enough for
compression, basically.

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:10.050
We will very
precisely characterize

00:27:10.050 --> 00:27:12.240
what is the best prefix code.

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:14.880
As we saw, Shannon code is
clearly not the best prefix

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:15.810
code.

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:17.050
But it's a good one.

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:17.550
OK.

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:19.530
And then, we will
justify the thumb rule,

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:24.040
which is that the lengths should
be roughly log 1/Px, right?

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:26.830
This log 1/Px will keep
coming again and again

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:27.970
throughout the course.

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:29.800
And if you have done
machine learning,

00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:31.630
you will see that it
even comes up there,

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:35.860
where you have these log
loss terms, and you have--

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:39.580
there was a recent paper
by DeepMind, the Google

00:27:39.580 --> 00:27:43.360
subsidiary, where the paper is
just called language modeling

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:44.500
compression.

00:27:44.500 --> 00:27:47.505
And we will see that in the
later homeworks, and so on.

00:27:47.505 --> 00:27:49.630
There is a very deep
connection between compression

00:27:49.630 --> 00:27:54.280
and modeling or
learning in general.

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:57.580
But, yeah, just this
basic thing will just

00:27:57.580 --> 00:28:01.260
keep talking about it.

00:28:01.260 --> 00:28:04.110
OK, so first, quantity entropy.

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:07.990
How many of you have
heard about entropy?

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:12.640
How many of you have heard about
entropy in a physics context?

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:14.980
Yeah, so many people are
familiar about entropy

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:17.680
from physics context.

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:20.570
I'm not done physics, so I don't
know what it exactly means.

00:28:20.570 --> 00:28:23.680
So I will tell you what
it means here, right?

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:27.190
So I think it's very
similar, conceptually.

00:28:27.190 --> 00:28:30.460
OK, so you have a
alphabet 1 to k.

00:28:30.460 --> 00:28:32.800
From now on, we will not
talk about A, B, C, D. It's

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:34.270
just harder to keep track of.

00:28:34.270 --> 00:28:38.580
Our alphabets will typically
be just 1, 2, 3, 4, k.

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:40.530
You have a probability
distribution over that.

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:42.720
And we will use this
shorthand notation.

00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:45.450
Pi will be the probability
that x is equal to i,

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:48.120
just to simplify our notation.

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:51.970
Then entropy is this quantity--

00:28:51.970 --> 00:28:54.400
sum of Pi log 1 by Pi.

00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:56.230
The unit of entropy is bits.

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:59.440
We will see why in a bit.

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:08.840
OK, we'll do some examples, and
then I will wait for questions.

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:11.470
So one thing you notice
is that entropy only

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:13.180
depends on the
probability distribution.

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:15.408
So entropy is really
not a function of x.

00:29:15.408 --> 00:29:17.200
It's not a function of
the random variable.

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:18.700
It's a function of
the distribution.

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:20.800
If you have two random variables
with the same distribution,

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:21.970
they have the same entropy.

00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:23.740
It's a function of
the distribution.

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:29.940
You can write the entropy
as an expectation, right?

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:36.240
Because you see here that--

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:37.740
you remember that
the expectation

00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:45.052
is expectation of some
y is summation Pi y

00:29:45.052 --> 00:29:45.885
Something like this.

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:52.890
OK.

00:29:52.890 --> 00:30:00.660
So here, like, it just Pi
times log 1 over Px equal to i.

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:02.910
Just by the definition
of expectation,

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:05.640
so it's just an easier
way to write it.

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:08.790
And third thing, if you
have actual questions

00:30:08.790 --> 00:30:10.998
on this, if you ever
run into a place

00:30:10.998 --> 00:30:12.540
where you want to
compute the entropy

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:15.060
or if you want to
write it in Python,

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:21.390
like a code to compute entropy,
when p is 0, this term is 0.

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:24.850
You can either assume it, or
you can use your calculus,

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:26.820
take the limit or
whatever you want to do,

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:28.380
but this is zero, right?

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:31.860
Otherwise, you run
into not a numbers

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:33.810
and so on in your Python script.

00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:36.210
So that's just
like a definition.

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:38.362
OK, any questions so far?

00:30:38.362 --> 00:30:39.320
We'll do some examples.

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:43.445
OK, let's do examples.

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:48.540
So you have a
uniform distribution.

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:50.580
So your x is uniform.

00:30:56.130 --> 00:31:00.780
So the x is uniform over 1 to k.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:04.110
So what that means is like
Px equal to i is just 1

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:07.140
over k for all i.

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:10.460
Then your entropy of x is--

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:12.470
just use the formula
from last slide,

00:31:12.470 --> 00:31:18.310
so P log 1 over P.
That's the formula.

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:28.350
And after you do the
math, you see log k bits.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:28.850
OK.

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:33.120
Is that clear?

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:40.250
Good.

00:31:40.250 --> 00:31:42.590
Have you seen log k
in any other context

00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:44.690
for uniform distributions
in the past?

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:50.330
In the last two
lectures, basically.

00:31:50.330 --> 00:31:51.770
Let me just remind
you, you might

00:31:51.770 --> 00:31:54.530
remember that log k is actually
the length of the fixed length

00:31:54.530 --> 00:31:56.270
code for--

00:31:56.270 --> 00:31:59.090
or I guess the ceiling of log k.

00:31:59.090 --> 00:31:59.603
Right.

00:31:59.603 --> 00:32:02.270
So you already see that there is
some connection between entropy

00:32:02.270 --> 00:32:07.610
and the length of a code, and
we will explore that shortly.

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:08.120
Right.

00:32:08.120 --> 00:32:14.650
So in particular, if x
is like Bernoulli half--

00:32:14.650 --> 00:32:16.600
so Bernoulli random
variables, this just

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:21.400
means that uniform
over 0 and 1--

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:28.975
then H of x is log2,
which is 1 bit.

00:32:28.975 --> 00:32:30.600
So if you have a
random variable, which

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:33.360
is either 0 or 1 with
equal probability,

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:38.760
then it has 1 bit of entropy.

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.250
Right.

00:32:40.250 --> 00:32:40.750
OK.

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:48.270
Let us compute it
for this one also.

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:51.550
Thus, we are very comfortable
with the calculations.

00:32:51.550 --> 00:32:55.390
So 1/2 log 1 over-- you
will have some questions

00:32:55.390 --> 00:32:58.300
in the homework-- in the
quiz and the homework--

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:06.840
times 2.

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:16.410
So this is 1/2 log1 plus
1/4 log2 plus 1/8 times 5

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:20.210
plus 1/8 times 3.

00:33:20.210 --> 00:33:35.950
So 1/2 plus 1/2 plus 6/8, 7/4,
according to helpful audience

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:36.610
members.

00:33:36.610 --> 00:33:37.300
OK.

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:44.780
So as an exercise,
which I would encourage

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:48.835
you to do at home, if
you have this code--

00:33:59.120 --> 00:34:03.800
if you create this code
for this distribution,

00:34:03.800 --> 00:34:05.480
then if you calculate
the expected

00:34:05.480 --> 00:34:08.940
length of the codewords.

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:10.530
Yeah, you just
compute it at home,

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:13.710
and you will find
that it's the entropy.

00:34:13.710 --> 00:34:16.350
So again, you see some
connection between the lengths,

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:18.405
average length of the
codewords, and the entropy.

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:23.219
OK, any questions about--

00:34:23.219 --> 00:34:25.679
for now, you should just know
that given a distribution,

00:34:25.679 --> 00:34:28.320
you follow this exact formula,
and you know the entropy.

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:29.980
That's it.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:31.455
And we'll see more shortly.

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:36.780
Properties of entropy.

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:43.000
So for a random variable
that takes up to k values,

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:47.080
the entropy is
between 0 and log k.

00:34:47.080 --> 00:34:48.595
We already saw when it's log k.

00:34:48.595 --> 00:34:52.120
It's log k when it's a
uniformly distributed.

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:56.050
And it is zero when
it is deterministic.

00:34:56.050 --> 00:34:58.450
By deterministic, we mean
that x takes exactly one

00:34:58.450 --> 00:35:01.420
value with probability
1, so it's not random.

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:02.980
It's deterministic.

00:35:02.980 --> 00:35:06.220
So again, for a
random variable that

00:35:06.220 --> 00:35:09.820
takes values in an
alphabet of size k,

00:35:09.820 --> 00:35:14.790
entropy is between 0 and log k.

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:15.720
I went to the proofs.

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:17.040
The zero proof is very easy.

00:35:17.040 --> 00:35:20.130
It just depends on the fact that
probabilities are less than 1.

00:35:20.130 --> 00:35:23.550
And the log k proof
uses convexity

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:24.750
and Jensen's inequality.

00:35:24.750 --> 00:35:26.820
So for those of you who
are familiar with that,

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:29.175
it's a few lines of math.

00:35:33.260 --> 00:35:36.810
OK, so let's just talk about
what entropy means and so on.

00:35:36.810 --> 00:35:38.660
So entropy is like--

00:35:38.660 --> 00:35:41.120
it's like a measure of
uncertainty or randomness

00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:43.790
in your random variable.

00:35:43.790 --> 00:35:45.980
So if your random
variable is deterministic,

00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:47.210
there is no randomness.

00:35:47.210 --> 00:35:49.570
Entropy is zero, right?

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:52.450
If a random variable is
uniform over all possibilities,

00:35:52.450 --> 00:35:54.760
then it has the
maximum entropy, right?

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:57.220
So the more random your
thing is, the more entropy

00:35:57.220 --> 00:35:58.810
it will have.

00:35:58.810 --> 00:35:59.380
Right.

00:35:59.380 --> 00:36:01.450
So that's just
like what it means.

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:04.300
Very similar meaning in physics.

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:06.220
Another way to think
about it is the entropy

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:09.790
is the amount of information
contained in a message.

00:36:09.790 --> 00:36:12.520
So for example, let's
say you have a friend who

00:36:12.520 --> 00:36:14.260
sends you messages.

00:36:14.260 --> 00:36:17.110
And all the messages
they send you are hello.

00:36:17.110 --> 00:36:19.220
They just send the same
message every time.

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:21.670
So when you receive the
message, it gives you

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:23.260
very little information, right?

00:36:23.260 --> 00:36:25.210
Some other person-- like,
somebody, sometimes,

00:36:25.210 --> 00:36:25.840
they say hello.

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:26.798
Sometimes, they say hi.

00:36:26.798 --> 00:36:28.720
So maybe you have some
information, right?

00:36:28.720 --> 00:36:33.250
So the more entropy you have,
the more variability you have,

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:37.360
more information you can
capture in that message.

00:36:37.360 --> 00:36:40.690
So entropy is the
amount of information

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:42.490
that's contained in a random.

00:36:42.490 --> 00:36:47.250
Deterministic random variable
has zero information.

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:50.130
And since at the end
of the day, compression

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:53.030
is about storing
the information,

00:36:53.030 --> 00:36:55.160
entropy is also how
much you can compress.

00:36:55.160 --> 00:36:57.170
And this, we'll see in
this class, that entropy

00:36:57.170 --> 00:36:58.640
is the limit of compression.

00:36:58.640 --> 00:37:02.250
For a given random
variable, the entropy

00:37:02.250 --> 00:37:04.080
is the best compression
you can achieve

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:05.940
for that random variable.

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:10.070
And that's why the unit is bits.

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:14.780
And if you have ever seen these
puzzles, like I have x coins,

00:37:14.780 --> 00:37:17.347
and then one of the coin is
lighter than the other coins,

00:37:17.347 --> 00:37:18.680
and one of the coins is heavier.

00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:20.097
And I have a
weighing scale, and I

00:37:20.097 --> 00:37:23.360
want to-- how many minimum
number of weighings

00:37:23.360 --> 00:37:27.860
should I do to find out the
heavier coins or the lighter

00:37:27.860 --> 00:37:29.680
coin?

00:37:29.680 --> 00:37:32.440
Or you have a bunch of
contestants in a tournament,

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:34.960
and then one of the
contestants is stronger.

00:37:34.960 --> 00:37:36.100
Everybody else is equal.

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:38.620
How many tournament
rounds do I need?

00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:40.360
So there are a bunch
of these puzzles.

00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:43.720
And many times, you will see the
answer is related to entropy.

00:37:43.720 --> 00:37:46.390
And you will see some of
those in our homeworks.

00:37:48.950 --> 00:37:51.380
Entropy is also important for
sampling from distributions,

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:55.210
so you have a-- you
have a fair coin,

00:37:55.210 --> 00:37:57.273
but you want to
simulate a biased coin.

00:37:57.273 --> 00:37:58.690
Or you have a
biased coin, and you

00:37:58.690 --> 00:38:00.700
want to simulate a fair coin.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:03.880
All these situations,
entropy comes up

00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:08.020
as the limit of things.

00:38:08.020 --> 00:38:10.360
I think we will see all
of these to some extent

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:14.550
in the homeworks, so I
won't spend class time.

00:38:14.550 --> 00:38:19.590
OK, any questions so far?

00:38:19.590 --> 00:38:21.680
So this is mostly
standard material.

00:38:21.680 --> 00:38:23.740
Let's keep going.

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:25.450
What is this?

00:38:25.450 --> 00:38:28.190
Joint entropy of independent
random variables.

00:38:28.190 --> 00:38:32.140
So if you have two random
variables, x1 and x2,

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:34.610
which are independent
of each other,

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:39.020
then you can define
their joint entropy.

00:38:39.020 --> 00:38:40.770
So one thing that's
useful to think about,

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:42.960
which, in math,
even programming,

00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:44.970
everywhere, right,
you have two things.

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:48.120
But x1 and x2 are
individual random variables,

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:49.050
but they're pair.

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:51.240
The tuple itself is
another random variable.

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:54.277
So the entropy of the tuple is
just the entropy of the tuple.

00:38:54.277 --> 00:38:56.610
You don't even need to worry
about the individual random

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:57.318
variables, right?

00:38:57.318 --> 00:38:58.860
You just think of
this pair as y.

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:04.190
I can just call this whole
thing as y, and then this is y.

00:39:04.190 --> 00:39:06.420
So just in terms of
thinking about, if you

00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:08.130
see a more complicated
object, just think

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:10.185
of that whole big
object as your y.

00:39:14.540 --> 00:39:17.780
OK, those who are
able to follow,

00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:20.330
how did I get the second
step from the first step?

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:25.810
Because the two random
variables are independent,

00:39:25.810 --> 00:39:28.780
so then the joint probability
is just the product.

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:29.280
Yep.

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:29.610
Yep.

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:30.390
Yep.
Yeah.

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:34.090
So correct answer is that since
x1 and x2 are independent,

00:39:34.090 --> 00:39:36.220
the joint probability
factor factors out

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:39.700
as the product of the
individual probabilities.

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:40.570
Great.

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:44.890
So we know this, and then
we know the log properties.

00:39:44.890 --> 00:39:48.560
If we have forgotten properties
of log, please revisit them.

00:39:48.560 --> 00:39:50.380
They will be helpful.

00:39:50.380 --> 00:39:51.430
Right.

00:39:51.430 --> 00:39:52.960
So at the end of
the day, you get

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:56.750
that the entropy of
the two variables

00:39:56.750 --> 00:39:58.460
is the same as the
sum of the entropy.

00:39:58.460 --> 00:40:02.300
So what we are saying is you
have two random variables

00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:03.110
are independent.

00:40:03.110 --> 00:40:05.810
So the total information
contained in them

00:40:05.810 --> 00:40:07.880
is the sum of their
individual informations

00:40:07.880 --> 00:40:09.260
because they are independent.

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:11.940
If they were not independent,
then something else

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:12.440
will happen.

00:40:12.440 --> 00:40:14.765
And we'll talk about
that in a future lecture.

00:40:23.220 --> 00:40:25.110
OK, so we already saw
the first statement,

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:26.670
that if x1 and x2
are independent,

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:31.210
then the joint entropy is
the sum of the entropies.

00:40:31.210 --> 00:40:35.630
How many of you have
heard the term iid?

00:40:35.630 --> 00:40:36.440
OK, that's good.

00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:36.940
Yeah.

00:40:36.940 --> 00:40:38.660
So iid is a very
common term, which

00:40:38.660 --> 00:40:41.420
means independent and
identically distributed, right?

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:43.490
So you have n variables.

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:45.830
They are all mutually
independent, so

00:40:45.830 --> 00:40:47.510
not only pairwise, but jointly.

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:49.970
They're all mutually
independent of each other.

00:40:49.970 --> 00:40:52.700
And each of them have
the same distribution.

00:40:52.700 --> 00:40:54.410
So we often use this notation.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:59.060
So what this is saying is xn
is an n-tuple of x1, dot, dot,

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:00.320
dot, xn.

00:41:00.320 --> 00:41:02.810
Each of them have the
same distribution as x.

00:41:02.810 --> 00:41:04.850
x is a placeholder
random variable,

00:41:04.850 --> 00:41:07.160
which is just denotes
the distribution of all

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:08.090
of these guys.

00:41:08.090 --> 00:41:11.090
So you are maybe tossing
a coin n times, right?

00:41:11.090 --> 00:41:13.670
So each of the coin toss is
an independent random variable

00:41:13.670 --> 00:41:16.790
with the same distribution,
with some Bernoulli

00:41:16.790 --> 00:41:21.890
half or Bernoulli P. So
then using the same thing

00:41:21.890 --> 00:41:24.800
inductively, you will get that
the entropy of the n-tuple

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:28.160
is n times the entropy of
a single random variable.

00:41:28.160 --> 00:41:29.892
So entropy just
adds up, if you have

00:41:29.892 --> 00:41:31.100
independent random variables.

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:32.720
If you don't have
independent, then we

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:35.460
will talk about it in lecture 8.

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:37.080
It's a very important use case.

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:40.310
But for now, let's
keep it simple.

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:41.900
Any questions so far?

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:48.180
OK.

00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:52.260
Another quantity.

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:52.980
Don't be scared.

00:41:52.980 --> 00:41:58.860
This is called KL divergence.

00:41:58.860 --> 00:42:02.777
So this is defined on two random
variables-- two probability

00:42:02.777 --> 00:42:03.360
distributions.

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:05.160
You have p, and you have q.

00:42:05.160 --> 00:42:08.670
Both are on the same alphabet,
so both are on 1 to k.

00:42:08.670 --> 00:42:12.150
So p is like p1,
p2, p3, pk, and then

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:16.110
q is like q1, q2, qk on
the same distribution.

00:42:16.110 --> 00:42:19.230
So then this
divergence or distance

00:42:19.230 --> 00:42:24.850
or relative entropy between p
and q is defined as this thing.

00:42:24.850 --> 00:42:27.520
I will not talk
about it for now.

00:42:27.520 --> 00:42:30.040
Just you will have
some examples in quiz.

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:33.190
Solve some questions, right?

00:42:33.190 --> 00:42:36.550
It's just something pi log
pi/qi, the sum of these things,

00:42:36.550 --> 00:42:37.540
right?

00:42:37.540 --> 00:42:39.910
Doesn't tell you much,
but the properties

00:42:39.910 --> 00:42:42.630
will tell us much more.

00:42:42.630 --> 00:42:45.000
OK.

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:46.080
OK.

00:42:46.080 --> 00:42:48.510
There is only one property
we need that it is positive.

00:42:48.510 --> 00:42:51.510
It's greater than or equal to
zero with equality if and only

00:42:51.510 --> 00:42:53.190
if p is equal to q.

00:42:53.190 --> 00:42:56.785
So it is in a way a distance
between two probability

00:42:56.785 --> 00:42:57.660
distributions, right?

00:42:57.660 --> 00:43:00.460
If the probability distributions
are similar, it's smaller.

00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:02.783
If they are not
similar, it's bigger.

00:43:02.783 --> 00:43:04.950
So it's a way to describe
the difference between two

00:43:04.950 --> 00:43:06.870
probability distributions.

00:43:06.870 --> 00:43:09.940
Proof is in lecture notes, uses
convexity Jensen's inequality

00:43:09.940 --> 00:43:10.440
again.

00:43:13.220 --> 00:43:14.210
Yeah.

00:43:14.210 --> 00:43:15.560
So just remember this.

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:18.470
We will just need this
property one time in our proof

00:43:18.470 --> 00:43:20.300
later so that's
why I described it.

00:43:23.460 --> 00:43:25.170
So, yeah, so KL divergence--

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:27.120
Kullback-Leibler,
the two people who

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:29.370
came up with it--
or relative entropy

00:43:29.370 --> 00:43:33.270
is a measure of distance between
two probability distributions.

00:43:33.270 --> 00:43:35.070
It is not symmetric.

00:43:35.070 --> 00:43:40.430
So when you think of your
loss, your functions,

00:43:40.430 --> 00:43:42.260
typically, like
distance functions,

00:43:42.260 --> 00:43:47.540
like Euclidean distance or l1,
l2, l0, something, most of them

00:43:47.540 --> 00:43:48.230
are symmetric.

00:43:48.230 --> 00:43:50.805
So between x and y, and y
and x, it doesn't matter.

00:43:50.805 --> 00:43:51.680
Here, it does matter.

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:54.140
You will see it in your quiz.

00:43:54.140 --> 00:44:03.080
So this is, in general,
it's not equal to D qp.

00:44:05.740 --> 00:44:07.570
It's fine.

00:44:07.570 --> 00:44:08.380
It's just a fact.

00:44:11.290 --> 00:44:13.540
How many of you have
seen it actually?

00:44:13.540 --> 00:44:15.940
Some of you might have
seen it in your machine

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:16.870
learning classes.

00:44:16.870 --> 00:44:19.570
If you have done
generative models,

00:44:19.570 --> 00:44:22.220
especially, this comes up a lot.

00:44:22.220 --> 00:44:23.470
And why does it come up a lot?

00:44:23.470 --> 00:44:25.690
Because this is a distance
between probability

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:26.590
distributions, right?

00:44:26.590 --> 00:44:28.090
So all of these
generative models,

00:44:28.090 --> 00:44:29.215
what are they trying to do?

00:44:29.215 --> 00:44:33.700
They're trying to your LLMs
and diffusion models and so on.

00:44:33.700 --> 00:44:37.420
They are trying to
generate samples,

00:44:37.420 --> 00:44:39.890
which are similar to the
real distribution, right?

00:44:39.890 --> 00:44:42.100
They're trying to
create human-like text.

00:44:42.100 --> 00:44:46.830
They are trying to create
artistic-like images.

00:44:46.830 --> 00:44:49.470
But to compute the
loss, like, how far

00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:51.175
they are from the real thing?

00:44:51.175 --> 00:44:52.800
You need to have a
loss function, which

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:55.830
determines how different two
probability distributions are.

00:44:55.830 --> 00:44:58.080
And for various reasons,
including the fact

00:44:58.080 --> 00:45:00.090
that it's differentiable
and everything,

00:45:00.090 --> 00:45:02.640
KL divergence is
the loss function

00:45:02.640 --> 00:45:04.470
of choice in many, many cases.

00:45:04.470 --> 00:45:07.990
So I was just mentioning it.

00:45:07.990 --> 00:45:09.310
You don't need it for now.

00:45:09.310 --> 00:45:10.540
But, yeah.

00:45:10.540 --> 00:45:13.300
And it has some
interpretation in compression,

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:15.490
which we might talk about.

00:45:15.490 --> 00:45:18.190
It's in the notes, definitely.

00:45:18.190 --> 00:45:18.690
OK.

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:19.773
We will move on from this.

00:45:19.773 --> 00:45:21.750
For now, just remember
this one thing

00:45:21.750 --> 00:45:24.450
that the KL divergence
is greater than or equal

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:26.430
to zero with
equality if and only

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:30.000
if the two distributions
are equal, right?

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:33.880
Rest of it is just
general knowledge.

00:45:33.880 --> 00:45:37.040
OK, so we are here.

00:45:37.040 --> 00:45:40.420
So the main result of
lossless compression, right?

00:45:40.420 --> 00:45:42.700
So information theory
is one of the fields

00:45:42.700 --> 00:45:45.910
where you will see results
which are very, very precise.

00:45:45.910 --> 00:45:48.010
You know exactly
what's happening.

00:45:48.010 --> 00:45:51.490
For every prefix
code in the world,

00:45:51.490 --> 00:45:56.050
the expected length is greater
than or equal to the entropy.

00:45:56.050 --> 00:45:59.590
There is no prefix code for
any distribution, right?

00:45:59.590 --> 00:46:01.780
There is no prefix code
that can beat entropy.

00:46:01.780 --> 00:46:03.370
You cannot go
better than entropy.

00:46:03.370 --> 00:46:05.770
Entropy is the
fundamental lower bound.

00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:07.870
So this result is
called a lower bound,

00:46:07.870 --> 00:46:09.040
or it's called a converse.

00:46:11.560 --> 00:46:12.760
And not only this.

00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:15.220
If I just give this and
then I didn't give you

00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:18.207
what can you do, this would
be-- this is a negative result,

00:46:18.207 --> 00:46:18.790
anyway, right?

00:46:18.790 --> 00:46:20.832
This just tells you can't
do better than entropy.

00:46:20.832 --> 00:46:24.220
But there is another result.
You can achieve entropy.

00:46:24.220 --> 00:46:28.300
You can get arbitrarily
close to entropy.

00:46:28.300 --> 00:46:31.360
And we will talk about
maybe today or next lecture

00:46:31.360 --> 00:46:35.190
about what do you mean
by arbitrarily close

00:46:35.190 --> 00:46:36.240
with prefix codes.

00:46:36.240 --> 00:46:39.810
So this is the
achievability part.

00:46:39.810 --> 00:46:42.060
I will stop here
for a minute and let

00:46:42.060 --> 00:47:05.160
you digest OK, any questions?

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:08.960
Good.

00:47:13.420 --> 00:47:13.920
Right.

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:15.070
So what does mean?

00:47:15.070 --> 00:47:17.070
This means that entropy
is the fundamental limit

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:19.080
of lossless compression.

00:47:19.080 --> 00:47:23.040
It's a very powerful result.
In the next several lectures,

00:47:23.040 --> 00:47:26.070
what we will be doing is trying
to actually achieve entropy,

00:47:26.070 --> 00:47:28.770
all of the work, basically,
lossless compression.

00:47:28.770 --> 00:47:32.110
So Shannon, in his 1948
paper, he showed this result.

00:47:32.110 --> 00:47:34.110
He showed that you can't
do better than entropy.

00:47:34.110 --> 00:47:39.480
And next 50 years-- even as
soon as like 2014 or something,

00:47:39.480 --> 00:47:42.390
people are still trying to
find more and more efficient

00:47:42.390 --> 00:47:44.430
ways to achieve entropy.

00:47:44.430 --> 00:47:49.020
All of your lossless
and lossy compressors,

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:50.820
at their very root,
have something

00:47:50.820 --> 00:47:52.470
called an entropy coder.

00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:54.270
Entropy coder is
something that is just

00:47:54.270 --> 00:47:58.290
trying to achieve the entropy
of whatever the input is.

00:47:58.290 --> 00:48:00.540
You have Huffman codes, and
you have arithmetic codes.

00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:01.260
You have ANS.

00:48:01.260 --> 00:48:03.930
You have this and a
bunch of different ideas.

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:06.030
All of these, trying
to just do this,

00:48:06.030 --> 00:48:08.550
try to achieve entropy
in an efficient way.

00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:13.790
So after Shannon
proved this result,

00:48:13.790 --> 00:48:17.127
I think it was almost
30 years before somebody

00:48:17.127 --> 00:48:18.710
was able to come up
with a method that

00:48:18.710 --> 00:48:23.660
was linear time, a linear time
in computational complexity

00:48:23.660 --> 00:48:25.880
way of achieving entropy.

00:48:25.880 --> 00:48:29.300
And then more recently
people have improved that.

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:34.280
It's still linear time, but
better for modern hardware.

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:38.870
OK, same result applies
to the more general class

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:40.190
of uniquely decodable codes.

00:48:40.190 --> 00:48:42.080
So if you noticed
in the last lecture,

00:48:42.080 --> 00:48:46.010
I said for every prefix code,
you cannot beat entropy.

00:48:46.010 --> 00:48:48.852
But actually, for any lossless
code, any uniquely decodable

00:48:48.852 --> 00:48:50.060
code, you can't beat entropy.

00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:51.893
Entropy is the fundamental
bound of lossless

00:48:51.893 --> 00:48:55.040
compression, whether or not
you want to use prefix codes.

00:48:55.040 --> 00:48:56.960
This, you will prove in--

00:48:56.960 --> 00:48:58.865
this part, you will
prove in the homework.

00:49:02.890 --> 00:49:06.550
And the last point is that, as
I mentioned a little bit before,

00:49:06.550 --> 00:49:09.610
we assume here that your things
are independent identically

00:49:09.610 --> 00:49:11.540
distributed.

00:49:11.540 --> 00:49:15.410
Don't think that this
is only useful in very

00:49:15.410 --> 00:49:16.160
specific settings.

00:49:16.160 --> 00:49:18.050
No, this is useful
everywhere, and we

00:49:18.050 --> 00:49:20.030
will see it again and again.

00:49:20.030 --> 00:49:21.650
However, more
specifically, we will

00:49:21.650 --> 00:49:25.160
study more general distributions
in lecture 8 to 10, so a bit

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:25.670
later.

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:27.860
For now, we'll just
stick to the iid case.

00:49:30.520 --> 00:49:32.050
OK, any questions so far?

00:49:32.050 --> 00:49:38.220
We'll now go to the
proof of this result.

00:49:38.220 --> 00:49:41.100
I think the proof is not as
exciting I think really want.

00:49:41.100 --> 00:49:44.280
If you understand this
result, the enormity of this,

00:49:44.280 --> 00:49:47.630
I think, that's the main
point, the proof-proof.

00:49:47.630 --> 00:49:49.488
We will do the proof.

00:49:49.488 --> 00:49:50.530
Some of you will like it.

00:49:50.530 --> 00:49:52.480
Some of you will not.

00:49:52.480 --> 00:49:55.720
OK, let's do the proof.

00:49:59.010 --> 00:50:01.280
Actually, proof will give
you something very useful,

00:50:01.280 --> 00:50:03.400
so let's do it.

00:50:03.400 --> 00:50:05.970
OK, so first, we will
prove the converse.

00:50:05.970 --> 00:50:08.310
Converse is that you cannot
do better than the entropy.

00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:12.950
Any prefix code in
the world, well,

00:50:12.950 --> 00:50:17.770
cannot have expected code length
that is lower than the entropy.

00:50:17.770 --> 00:50:19.840
So we start with
our usual regime.

00:50:19.840 --> 00:50:23.680
We assume that x is distributed
according to some distribution

00:50:23.680 --> 00:50:24.430
p.

00:50:24.430 --> 00:50:27.640
And you have a prefix
code with certain lengths.

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:29.720
And what we will try
to show is that--

00:50:29.720 --> 00:50:32.010
so what are we trying to show?

00:50:32.010 --> 00:50:34.390
Let's write it down so
that it's very explicit.

00:50:34.390 --> 00:50:43.220
We are trying to show that
summation i equal to 1 to k,

00:50:43.220 --> 00:50:50.035
pi li is greater than or
equal to the entropy of p.

00:50:50.035 --> 00:50:50.535
OK.

00:50:55.120 --> 00:50:57.233
OK, so now some of the
proof will be very like,

00:50:57.233 --> 00:50:58.150
why are we doing this?

00:50:58.150 --> 00:51:00.380
But we will do it anyway.

00:51:00.380 --> 00:51:04.560
So now let's define qi as
another distribution, where

00:51:04.560 --> 00:51:11.320
qi is defined as c
times 2 power minus li,

00:51:11.320 --> 00:51:16.140
where c is defined
as this quantity.

00:51:16.140 --> 00:51:21.210
So here, you see I wrote that
c is equal to 1 over summation

00:51:21.210 --> 00:51:23.880
to power minus li is
greater than or equal to 1.

00:51:23.880 --> 00:51:26.250
Can somebody explain
why this holds?

00:51:31.640 --> 00:51:35.218
Isn't that just like from
the Kraft's inequality?

00:51:35.218 --> 00:51:36.500
This one over that?

00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:37.010
Yes.

00:51:37.010 --> 00:51:37.190
Yes.

00:51:37.190 --> 00:51:39.770
So the answer is that this
is Kraft's inequality, right?

00:51:39.770 --> 00:51:42.890
So we said that we will
prove this for prefix codes.

00:51:42.890 --> 00:51:45.140
But somehow, somewhere,
we need to use the fact

00:51:45.140 --> 00:51:46.730
that it's a prefix code, right?

00:51:46.730 --> 00:51:48.860
It's clearly not
true for general.

00:51:48.860 --> 00:51:50.342
If you didn't want
a lossless code,

00:51:50.342 --> 00:51:52.550
you could just have a code
where every length is zero

00:51:52.550 --> 00:51:54.080
or every length is one.

00:51:54.080 --> 00:51:57.830
So lossless codes can't
be better than entropy.

00:51:57.830 --> 00:52:02.300
You're like, non-lossless codes
can be better than entropy.

00:52:02.300 --> 00:52:06.350
So this is where we will use the
fact that it is a prefix code.

00:52:06.350 --> 00:52:09.387
A prefix code, we saw that
it's like an if and only

00:52:09.387 --> 00:52:10.220
if condition, right?

00:52:10.220 --> 00:52:12.650
Prefix codes if and only
if Kraft's inequality.

00:52:12.650 --> 00:52:14.930
So we use this here.

00:52:14.930 --> 00:52:16.330
OK.

00:52:16.330 --> 00:52:17.860
And then we do a
quick check, right?

00:52:17.860 --> 00:52:20.560
The c is a normalization
factor, right?

00:52:20.560 --> 00:52:23.260
Oftentimes, you have
something, and then you

00:52:23.260 --> 00:52:25.450
want to convert it into a
probability distribution.

00:52:25.450 --> 00:52:28.838
So you divide it by the sum so
that it becomes a probability

00:52:28.838 --> 00:52:29.380
distribution.

00:52:29.380 --> 00:52:30.670
So that's what we did here.

00:52:30.670 --> 00:52:34.450
So summation of q is just like
this thing, where you can--

00:52:34.450 --> 00:52:36.340
after you substitute
the value of c,

00:52:36.340 --> 00:52:39.340
you will very easily check
that the sum is actually one

00:52:39.340 --> 00:52:42.320
because the terms
will cancel out.

00:52:42.320 --> 00:52:45.410
OK, so with all
this, what we did

00:52:45.410 --> 00:52:47.810
was we already had a
probability distribution p.

00:52:47.810 --> 00:52:49.910
And now, we created a new
probability distribution

00:52:49.910 --> 00:52:54.520
q, where q has all
these lengths inside it.

00:52:54.520 --> 00:52:56.380
So now we have
two distributions,

00:52:56.380 --> 00:53:00.760
and we just read about a way
of using two distributions.

00:53:00.760 --> 00:53:04.330
We will use the KL divergence.

00:53:04.330 --> 00:53:07.690
And we already saw the
property that KL divergence is

00:53:07.690 --> 00:53:09.130
greater than or equal to zero.

00:53:09.130 --> 00:53:11.500
So let's just use it.

00:53:11.500 --> 00:53:16.090
And again, I didn't prove this
quantity, but I'm not cheating.

00:53:16.090 --> 00:53:17.533
You can read it.

00:53:17.533 --> 00:53:18.700
It's like a four-line proof.

00:53:18.700 --> 00:53:21.790
You just need to know Jensen's
inequality from convexity.

00:53:21.790 --> 00:53:25.240
