There is a gap.

01:08:25.430 --> 01:08:27.804
There is a gap from
entropy to Shannon codes.

01:08:30.910 --> 01:08:33.370
Do you think this code is
optimal for this distribution?

01:08:38.120 --> 01:08:39.490
Let me make a new slide.

01:08:39.490 --> 01:08:40.510
Let me ask you.

01:08:43.029 --> 01:08:45.670
So yeah, let's do
this properly so

01:08:45.670 --> 01:08:46.930
that we are on the same page.

01:08:46.930 --> 01:08:48.340
So we have this.

01:08:48.340 --> 01:08:49.300
So we have x.

01:08:49.300 --> 01:08:51.189
We have p of x.

01:08:51.189 --> 01:08:56.189
We have log 1 over p of x.

01:08:56.189 --> 01:08:59.170
So 2, 2, 2.

01:08:59.170 --> 01:09:06.950
And then we made this codeword,
cx, which is like 00, 01, 10,

01:09:06.950 --> 01:09:10.729
we can make a better
codeword, right?

01:09:10.729 --> 01:09:12.470
Better code.

01:09:12.470 --> 01:09:17.470
And I guess for the
code what we saw,

01:09:17.470 --> 01:09:22.590
for this the expected
code length was 2.

01:09:22.590 --> 01:09:24.710
Let's make a better code.

01:09:24.710 --> 01:09:28.270
So if we draw--

01:09:28.270 --> 01:09:30.100
let me just make it.

01:09:30.100 --> 01:09:32.439
So it's 00, 01.

01:09:32.439 --> 01:09:34.689
We don't need to
have this 0 after 1.

01:09:34.689 --> 01:09:36.189
I can just make it a 1.

01:09:36.189 --> 01:09:39.960
This is still prefix-free.

01:09:39.960 --> 01:09:44.830
And we computed entropy
was 1.58 before.

01:09:44.830 --> 01:09:46.120
So what is the entropy?

01:09:46.120 --> 01:09:48.430
What is the expected
code length of this guy?

01:09:48.430 --> 01:09:49.390
00, 01, 1.

01:09:55.880 --> 01:09:57.840
A few people say 1.67.

01:09:57.840 --> 01:09:59.610
Let me just check.

01:09:59.610 --> 01:10:04.770
So it's 2/3 plus 2/3 plus 1/3.

01:10:04.770 --> 01:10:08.980
So yeah, it's 1.67.

01:10:08.980 --> 01:10:10.770
Nice.

01:10:10.770 --> 01:10:12.060
See, we got close.

01:10:12.060 --> 01:10:14.490
We got closer.

01:10:14.490 --> 01:10:15.330
We were at 2 bits.

01:10:15.330 --> 01:10:18.270
Now, we are at 1.67.

01:10:18.270 --> 01:10:22.230
So this code that you
see on the far right,

01:10:22.230 --> 01:10:25.020
it's a Huffman code
for this distribution,

01:10:25.020 --> 01:10:26.760
and that's our next lecture.

01:10:26.760 --> 01:10:29.310
It is the optimum code
for this distribution.

01:10:29.310 --> 01:10:30.615
You cannot do better than this.

01:10:34.130 --> 01:10:36.980
But it's not still at entropy
you might observe, right?

01:10:36.980 --> 01:10:38.990
Still, 1.67 versus 1.5.

01:10:38.990 --> 01:10:40.280
There is a gap.

01:10:40.280 --> 01:10:44.970
Anybody has ideas how
we can remove this gap?

01:10:44.970 --> 01:10:49.020
Is it like for every
epsilon that you--

01:10:49.020 --> 01:10:51.420
for like every
degree of closeness,

01:10:51.420 --> 01:10:54.630
there exists a length
of your alphabet,

01:10:54.630 --> 01:10:58.230
so you would have to have
a much longer alphabet?

01:10:58.230 --> 01:11:00.030
But this is the
alphabet you are given.

01:11:00.030 --> 01:11:03.060
How can you create a bigger
alphabet starting from this?

01:11:06.400 --> 01:11:07.690
I see a few residents.

01:11:11.140 --> 01:11:11.650
Yes.

01:11:11.650 --> 01:11:12.150
Yes.

01:11:12.150 --> 01:11:12.827
So, yeah.

01:11:12.827 --> 01:11:14.410
So both of you are
on the right track.

01:11:14.410 --> 01:11:15.702
Basically, block coding, right?

01:11:15.702 --> 01:11:17.950
You need a bigger alphabet
in this case, right?

01:11:17.950 --> 01:11:20.200
This alphabet, you
can't do better, right?

01:11:20.200 --> 01:11:23.910
There is only so much, like,
with one symbol you can do.

01:11:23.910 --> 01:11:25.860
What you saw in homework
1, if you remember,

01:11:25.860 --> 01:11:29.148
you had a uniform fixed
length code over nine symbols,

01:11:29.148 --> 01:11:30.690
and then you made
it over 81 symbols.

01:11:30.690 --> 01:11:32.940
And you got better, and then
you can keep increasing it.

01:11:32.940 --> 01:11:34.800
And the result will be
something like that.

01:11:34.800 --> 01:11:38.220
For every epsilon, there
exists a block size n, such

01:11:38.220 --> 01:11:41.460
that you can get within
epsilon gap of the entropy.

01:11:41.460 --> 01:11:44.310
So of that's what we mean
by arbitrarily close.

01:11:46.960 --> 01:11:48.130
Right.

01:11:48.130 --> 01:11:51.850
So clearly, we are
not at achievability.

01:11:51.850 --> 01:11:53.590
We are not there.

01:11:53.590 --> 01:11:57.810
So this thing, we will
solve with Huffman code.

01:11:57.810 --> 01:12:00.930
We saw that Shannon codes
are sometimes just bad.

01:12:00.930 --> 01:12:02.670
And you will see an
example in the quiz

01:12:02.670 --> 01:12:06.000
today, where they're
like absurdly bad.

01:12:06.000 --> 01:12:08.370
So Huffman code is
the solution here.

01:12:08.370 --> 01:12:10.185
And you will see that
in the next lecture.

01:12:13.490 --> 01:12:15.260
Somebody suggested
block codes, right?

01:12:15.260 --> 01:12:16.790
And block codes are good, right?

01:12:16.790 --> 01:12:19.430
So they can achieve entropy.

01:12:19.430 --> 01:12:22.100
You can show that
they achieve entropy.

01:12:22.100 --> 01:12:24.950
We'll probably do that
early in the next lecture.

01:12:27.530 --> 01:12:30.860
But there are issues
with block codes.

01:12:30.860 --> 01:12:32.503
It just gets exponential.

01:12:32.503 --> 01:12:33.920
Bigger and bigger
blocks, you have

01:12:33.920 --> 01:12:35.600
to keep around
these huge tables.

01:12:35.600 --> 01:12:36.860
Its exponential complexity.

01:12:36.860 --> 01:12:38.180
It's not great.

01:12:38.180 --> 01:12:43.660
So we'll first look
at block codes.

01:12:43.660 --> 01:12:46.420
Then, we will look at something
called arithmetic codes, which

01:12:46.420 --> 01:12:50.073
get the same result, but
much more efficiently.

01:12:50.073 --> 01:12:51.490
And then we will
look at something

01:12:51.490 --> 01:12:54.040
called ANS, which, again,
gets the same result but even

01:12:54.040 --> 01:12:55.390
more efficiently.

01:12:55.390 --> 01:12:58.180
So there is a sequence
of codes, all of them

01:12:58.180 --> 01:13:01.510
used in a bunch of
real-life data compressors,

01:13:01.510 --> 01:13:05.380
themselves data compressors.

01:13:05.380 --> 01:13:06.160
Yeah.

01:13:06.160 --> 01:13:09.340
So this will be like the
plan for the next two weeks,

01:13:09.340 --> 01:13:12.310
basically, to look at
all these different codes

01:13:12.310 --> 01:13:15.317
and understand the there
are various very cool ideas.

01:13:15.317 --> 01:13:16.900
Like Huffman codes
are based on trees.

01:13:16.900 --> 01:13:19.660
Arithmetic codes are
based on arithmetic,

01:13:19.660 --> 01:13:22.540
and S codes are based on some
number systems and so on.

01:13:25.240 --> 01:13:26.530
We have 4 minutes.

01:13:26.530 --> 01:13:29.650
Let me do something
on block codes,

01:13:29.650 --> 01:13:30.940
unless we have questions.

01:13:37.070 --> 01:13:38.030
OK.

01:13:38.030 --> 01:13:39.350
OK.

01:13:39.350 --> 01:13:41.180
This will be very
short and simple.

01:13:41.180 --> 01:13:44.667
And then, we will do some
examples in the next lecture.

01:13:44.667 --> 01:13:47.250
This is just so that you go home
satisfied that we have proved

01:13:47.250 --> 01:13:48.720
that you can achieve entropy.

01:13:48.720 --> 01:14:01.700
OK, so if you code in blocks
of n symbols, what will happen?

01:14:01.700 --> 01:14:11.030
So if you remember
that the entropy of xn

01:14:11.030 --> 01:14:13.970
was-- if you have symbols, you
have independent identically

01:14:13.970 --> 01:14:15.080
distributed symbols.

01:14:15.080 --> 01:14:18.830
So the entropy of
xn was n times H(x).

01:14:18.830 --> 01:14:20.570
I hope everybody remembers.

01:14:20.570 --> 01:14:28.010
OK, now the Shannon
code on n symbols

01:14:28.010 --> 01:14:29.630
will obviously be
bigger than entropy.

01:14:29.630 --> 01:14:30.950
You cannot beat entropy.

01:14:30.950 --> 01:14:36.350
And it's less than
the entropy Xn plus 1.

01:14:36.350 --> 01:14:38.500
Yep.

01:14:38.500 --> 01:14:41.770
Let me write down this thing.

01:14:45.230 --> 01:14:50.970
I expanded the entropy term.

01:14:50.970 --> 01:14:52.517
Let me divide by n, right?

01:14:52.517 --> 01:14:54.600
Because what we care about
is per symbol, how much

01:14:54.600 --> 01:14:55.710
I'm paying, not per block.

01:14:55.710 --> 01:14:58.290
So per symbol is like
divided by n because I

01:14:58.290 --> 01:14:59.595
have a block of n symbols.

01:15:09.190 --> 01:15:11.950
Then, that's it basically--
if you work in blocks of 10,

01:15:11.950 --> 01:15:13.840
you can get within
1/n of entropy.

01:15:18.350 --> 01:15:18.850
Yeah.

01:15:21.940 --> 01:15:24.470
Before we go, let's quickly
look at this example.

01:15:24.470 --> 01:15:27.793
I think this might be
interesting, right?

01:15:27.793 --> 01:15:30.210
Actually, we didn't block--
we're doing blocks at all, OK?

01:15:30.210 --> 01:15:31.550
So this is not good.

01:15:31.550 --> 01:15:33.690
Next lecture, we'll
do an example.

01:15:33.690 --> 01:15:38.670
But everybody able
to follow this?

01:15:38.670 --> 01:15:43.050
So we basically code
in blocks of n symbols.

01:15:43.050 --> 01:15:45.120
So what you do is
you take n symbols,

01:15:45.120 --> 01:15:47.610
like you did in the first quiz.

01:15:47.610 --> 01:15:50.400
And you code over that
joint alphabet, right?

01:15:50.400 --> 01:15:52.230
So if you had A, B, C before.

01:15:52.230 --> 01:15:55.890
Now, you have AA, AB,
AC, dot, dot, dot, right?

01:15:55.890 --> 01:15:57.030
You have nine symbols.

01:15:57.030 --> 01:16:01.000
You make a Shannon code you make
a big tree with nine symbols.

01:16:01.000 --> 01:16:04.890
Then you code those
two at a time, right?

01:16:04.890 --> 01:16:07.670
Two at a time.

01:16:07.670 --> 01:16:10.980
And Shannon code, the property
holds for any distribution,

01:16:10.980 --> 01:16:11.480
right?

01:16:11.480 --> 01:16:13.370
It will hold for the
joint distribution also.

01:16:13.370 --> 01:16:15.110
It will hold for
the n-tuple also.

01:16:15.110 --> 01:16:18.140
The same property, this
property that we proved before,

01:16:18.140 --> 01:16:21.440
that you are between
x and x plus 1, that

01:16:21.440 --> 01:16:23.720
holds for arbitrary things.

01:16:23.720 --> 01:16:26.720
And then you divide by n, and
you get basically this result,

01:16:26.720 --> 01:16:30.840
that you can get
within one by n of.

01:16:30.840 --> 01:16:39.270
So within 1/n of entropy.

01:16:39.270 --> 01:16:43.710
So this is what we call
arbitrarily close, right?

01:16:43.710 --> 01:16:47.660
So this is the proof of
the achievability part.

01:16:47.660 --> 01:16:49.990
Yep.

01:16:49.990 --> 01:16:53.610
OK, happy to answer
any questions.

01:16:53.610 --> 01:16:56.390
It's my office hour,
I believe, today.

